{-
   What a value moved into a literal is moved *across*.

   Both folds in codegen/js/opt.cpp - `foldInitializers` for an object and `foldArrayElements` for an
   array - rewrite `var v = {a: 0}; v.a = x;` into `var v = {a: x};`, and both walk past statements in
   between, because what a write needs is routinely declared there. The write moves **up**, so three
   things it crossed have to be checked, and each function below removes one of them:

   - `crossesEffect` puts an effectful statement between the literal and the write. Folding would run
     the write's own effect first, so the two counters come back in the wrong order;
   - `crossesBinding` puts a *declaration* the write's value reads between the two. Folding would put
     the read above the `var`, which is hoisted but unassigned - so the field holds `undefined`
     rather than the program failing;
   - `folds` has nothing in the way and is what the rules are for.

   Every one of these is invisible in the shape: a fold that reorders two effects still emits a
   program that runs, and one that reads a hoisted `var` emits `undefined` and keeps going. So the
   assertion is a number, and it is the *order* two calls ran in - see `log`, which is a decimal
   place per call rather than a sum, so that swapping two of them changes the answer.

   **Which of the three actually bites.** The binding guard does, and widely: disabling it fails
   `Box`, `LazyUser` and `ClosureHeaderDead` as well as this fixture. The *effect* guard is not
   reachable by any program in this corpus - the fold crosses a non-inert prefix thirty times, and
   the value it moves is a name or a constant every one of them, because the emitter names every
   instruction's result. That is a property of the emitter rather than a guarantee, which is exactly
   what `foldArrayElements` records about the same three checks; the case is written out here so the
   rule is stated somewhere a reader will find it, not because a fixture can fail on it.
-}
let &log = 0 :: Int

data Pair {a: Int, b: Int}

-- Appends `n` as the next decimal digit, so the answer records the order the calls ran in and not
-- only which of them did.
--
-- Self-recursive, and that is what makes the fixture bite rather than a flourish: an inlined `note`
-- is split into an effect statement and a constant, the constant has no effects to cross, and both
-- guards below become vacuous. The recursion is unreachable - `log` never gets that large - so it
-- costs the answer nothing and costs the inliner the call.
fn note(n: Int) -> Int:
    log = log * 10 + n
    if log > 1000000000:
        return note(0)
    return n

-- An effect between the literal and the write. `note(1)` has to stay in front of `note(2)`.
fn crossesEffect() -> Int:
    let &p = Pair {a: 0, b: 9}
    let first = note(1)
    p.a = note(2)
    return p.a * 10 + p.b + first

-- The write's value reads a binding declared after the literal, so it cannot move above it.
fn crossesBinding() -> Int:
    let &p = Pair {a: 0, b: 8}
    let x = note(3)
    p.a = x + 1
    return p.a * 10 + p.b

-- The same for an array literal, which is the other fold.
fn elementCrossesEffect() -> Int:
    let &xs = [0, 7]
    let first = note(4)
    xs[0] = note(5)
    return xs[0] * 10 + xs[1] + first

-- Nothing in the way, which is what both rules are for.
fn folds() -> Int:
    let &p = Pair {a: 0, b: 6}
    p.a = 5
    return p.a * 10 + p.b

fn main() -> Int = crossesEffect() + crossesBinding() + elementCrossesEffect() + folds() + log
